% lib/temporal.le — dates, periods and lock times, as LE templates.
%
% Extension E3 of InsurLE2/docs/MiggratingFromOtherSystems.md (§7.2): the
% temporal and period LIBRARY the migrations share (Oracle Intelligent Advisor
% rules that quantify over time, effective-dated product versions, Bitcoin
% timelocks), so that no translator re-derives the same rules. A library, not
% syntax: include it as a resource (a translator copies it beside the program
% it writes, with temporal.pl), and use its templates like any other.
%
% LE itself already has `*a date* is *a number* days after *a date*`,
% `... months after ...` and the date comparisons (`is after`, `is before or
% equal to`, ...); this library adds what those do not give.
%
% What is NOT here: Oracle Intelligent Advisor's change-point semantics
% (attributes as step functions recomputed over time). A rulebase that leans
% on them is marked "approximated" in its migration ledger.

the knowledge base temporal includes these resources:
    temporal.pl.

the templates are:
    the age on *a date* of someone born on *a birth date* is *a number*.
    the number of whole years from *a date* to *a second date* is *a number*.
    the number of whole months from *a date* to *a second date* is *a number*.
    the number of days from *a date* to *a second date* is *a number*.
    *a later date* is *a number* years after *a date*.
    *a later date* is *a number* calendar months after *a date*.
    *an earlier date* is *a number* calendar months before *a date*.
    *a date* is in the period from *a start date* to *an end date*.
    *a date* is within the last *a number* months before *a reference date*.
    *a date* is within the last *a number* days before *a reference date*.
    the first day of the month of *a date* is *a first date*.
    the last day of the month of *a date* is *a last date*.
    the year of *a date* is *a number*.
    the month of *a date* is *a number*.
    the weekday of *a date* is *a number*.
    *a date* is in a leap year.
    the lock time *a number* is a block height.
    the lock time *a number* is a time.
    block *a height* is at least *a number* blocks after block *a first height*.

the knowledge base temporal includes:

the age on a date of someone born on a birth date is a number N if
    prolog le_temporal_years_between(the birth date, the date, N).

the number of whole years from a date to a second date is a number N if
    prolog le_temporal_years_between(the date, the second date, N).

the number of whole months from a date to a second date is a number N if
    prolog le_temporal_months_between(the date, the second date, N).

the number of days from a date to a second date is a number N if
    prolog le_temporal_days_between(the date, the second date, N).

a later date is a number N years after a date if
    prolog le_temporal_add_years(the date, N, the later date).

a later date is a number N calendar months after a date if
    prolog le_temporal_add_months(the date, N, the later date).

an earlier date is a number N calendar months before a date if
    prolog le_temporal_sub_months(the date, N, the earlier date).

a date is in the period from a start date to an end date if
    prolog le_temporal_in_period(the date, the start date, the end date).

a date is within the last a number N months before a reference date if
    prolog le_temporal_within_months_before(the date, N, the reference date).

a date is within the last a number N days before a reference date if
    prolog le_temporal_within_days_before(the date, N, the reference date).

the first day of the month of a date is a first date if
    prolog le_temporal_month_start(the date, the first date).

the last day of the month of a date is a last date if
    prolog le_temporal_month_end(the date, the last date).

the year of a date is a number N if
    prolog le_temporal_year(the date, N).

the month of a date is a number N if
    prolog le_temporal_month(the date, N).

the weekday of a date is a number N if
    prolog le_temporal_weekday(the date, N).

a date is in a leap year if
    prolog (le_temporal_year(the date, Y), le_temporal_leap(Y)).

the lock time a number N is a block height if
    prolog le_temporal_lock_is_height(N).

the lock time a number N is a time if
    prolog le_temporal_lock_is_time(N).

block a height H is at least a number N blocks after block a first height F if
    H - F >= N.
